      <h2 id="why">Why aroma type is the right map</h2>
      <p>Most spirits are classified by raw material or origin. Whisky is single malt or grain, Scotch or bourbon. Cognac is a region. Baijiu is classified by <em>aroma</em>, because aroma is what actually varies — and it varies enormously.</p>
      <p>The reason goes back to fermentation. Baijiu ferments grain in a solid state with a microbial starter called <em>qu</em>, and the type of qu, the temperature it reaches, the vessel it ferments in, and the length of the cycle together determine which aromatic compounds dominate. A high-temperature qu in a stone pit produces savoury, roasted, soy-like notes. A low-temperature qu in an earthenware jar produces clean pear and grain. Same grain, same country, completely different drink.</p>
      <p>So when a bottle says "sauce aroma," it is not describing a flavouring. It is describing an entire production system, and the flavour follows from it. That is why <a href="/db/">our database</a> is indexed by aroma type first.</p>

