      <p>The rest of this page covers the six types you are likely to encounter, in order of how much they matter to a buyer.</p>

      <h2 id="strong">Strong aroma — 浓香</h2>
      <p><strong>Standard:</strong> GB/T 10781.1 · <strong>Share of the category:</strong> roughly half of all baijiu sold in China</p>
      <p>Strong aroma is the workhorse of Chinese drinking. If a restaurant in Chengdu or a wedding banquet in Nanjing serves baijiu, it is probably strong aroma. The style ferments in mud pits — the pit wall carries a microbial community built up over decades, and that community produces the signature "cellar" note that sits underneath the fruit.</p>
      <p>On the nose you get ripe pineapple, honey, cooked grain, and a savoury earthiness that tasters call <em>jiaoxiang</em> (窖香, pit aroma). On the palate it is full-bodied and round, often with a sweetness that makes it the most immediately likeable of the major types. The finish is warm and long but rarely aggressive.</p>
      <p>This is the style to choose for a dinner with rich food, for a gift that needs to be universally appreciated, and for a first serious bottle. It is also the most competitive category in China, which means the quality floor is high and the value is good at almost every price point.</p>
