      <p>Within strong aroma there are regional sub-styles worth knowing. Sichuan versions are the fullest and most aromatic. Jiangsu versions — Yanghe above all — are deliberately softer, lower in ABV, and built for long meals. Anhui versions sit between the two, with more floral lift. None is "correct"; they are different answers to the same question.</p>

      <h2 id="sauce">Sauce aroma — 酱香</h2>
      <p><strong>Standard:</strong> GB/T 26760 (Moutai itself uses GB/T 18356, a geographical-indication standard)</p>
      <p>Sauce aroma is the most famous and the most demanding. The name refers to <em>jiang</em> (酱) — fermented bean paste — and that is genuinely what it smells like: soy, roasted grain, dried mushroom, toasted nuts, a hint of dried fruit, sometimes sandalwood or cocoa.</p>
      <p>The production cycle is the most laborious in the category. Sauce aroma runs a full year per batch: two grain additions, nine steamings, eight fermentations, seven distillate collections — the process the industry calls <em>12987</em>. Base spirit then ages at least three years before blending, and premium bottlings rest longer. This is why sauce aroma costs more across the board, independent of brand prestige.</p>
      <p>For a beginner, sauce aroma is a gamble. Drinkers who love peated Islay whisky, mezcal, aged sherry, or funky rum tend to find it immediately compelling. Drinkers who prefer clean spirits often find it medicinal or overwhelming on first contact. Both reactions are legitimate — this is a genuinely divisive flavour, not a question of sophistication.</p>
      <div class="callout"><strong>If you only try one sauce-aroma bottle:</strong> skip Moutai and buy <a href="/db/variety/xijiu/">Xijiu</a> or <a href="/db/variety/langjiu/">Langjiu</a> at $30–60. Both are serious sauce-aroma producers, both are far easier to authenticate, and both will tell you honestly whether you like the style before you spend $150+.</div>

      <h2 id="light">Light aroma — 清香</h2>
      <p><strong>Standard:</strong> GB/T 10781.2 · <strong>Benchmark:</strong> <a href="/db/variety/fenjiu/">Fenjiu</a>, Shanxi</p>
      <p>Light aroma is the cleanest expression of the category and the easiest entry point for a palate trained on Western spirits. It ferments in earthenware jars set into the ground, separated from direct soil contact, using low-temperature qu — a method that keeps the microbial action controlled and the flavour narrow.</p>
      <p>The result is dry, grain-forward, and precise: steamed rice, pear, a faint floral lift, and a finish that stops cleanly rather than lingering. "Light" refers to aromatic density, not to weakness. A 53% light aroma is a firm, structured drink.</p>
      <p>Fenjiu is the benchmark and one of the best-value bottles in the entire category — its flagship sells for around $15–40 depending on market. Beijing's <a href="/db/variety/niulanshan/">Niulanshan</a> and <a href="/db/variety/hongxing/">Hongxing Erguotou</a> are the everyday versions: cheap, bracing, and enormously popular in the north.</p>

      <h2 id="rice">Rice aroma — 米香</h2>
      <p><strong>Standard:</strong> GB/T 10781.3</p>
      <p>Rice aroma is the smallest of the six major types and the softest. It uses small qu (小曲) and a semi-solid fermentation, producing a spirit that is gentle, lightly sweet, and floral rather than savoury. Guilin Sanhua is the standard-bearer.</p>
      <p>Its weakness is also its strength: because it lacks intensity, it does not stand up to bold food or carry the prestige that gifting demands. But it is the most forgiving baijiu for a first taste, and it is the best candidate for cocktails, where its softness lets other ingredients speak. If you want to introduce someone to baijiu without scaring them, start here.</p>

      <h2 id="smaller">Phoenix, herbal, and mixed</h2>
      <h3>Phoenix aroma — 凤香 · GB/T 14867</h3>
      <p>Shaanxi's <a href="/db/variety/xifeng/">Xifeng</a> is the sole significant producer. The style ferments in a distinctive earthen pit that is renewed annually, which gives it a profile between light and strong: dry, slightly smoky, firm on the grain, with less fruit than a Sichuan strong aroma. It is one of the best-value bottles in the database at around $10–30, and a genuinely interesting stop for anyone exploring the category.</p>
      <h3>Herbal / Dong aroma — 董香 · DB52/T 550</h3>
      <p><a href="/db/variety/dongjiu/">Dongjiu</a> is the outlier of Chinese baijiu. It incorporates over a hundred medicinal herbs into the qu, producing a profile that is herbal, camphor-like, faintly bitter, and unlike anything else in the category. It is regulated under a Guizhou provincial standard rather than a national one, which tells you how singular it is. Try it out of curiosity, not as a first bottle.</p>
      <h3>Mixed aroma — 兼香 · GB/T 23547</h3>
      <p>Mixed aroma deliberately combines production methods to land between categories: typically a strong-aroma opening with a sauce-aroma finish. <a href="/db/variety/jiugui/">Jiugui</a> from Hunan is the best-known example, and its premium line is separately classified as fuyu aroma (馥郁香, GB/T 10781.11) because it layers three aroma profiles in sequence. These bottles are interesting precisely because they refuse to sit still.</p>

      <h2 id="rare">The four rare types</h2>
      <p>You will rarely meet these outside China, but they are worth knowing by name because they explain why the category has twelve entries rather than six.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Special aroma (特香, GB/T 20823)</strong> — Jiangxi's Site Liquor. Rice-husk-inoculated fermentation that produces a rice-grain base with strong-aroma depth. Unusual and rewarding.</li>
        <li><strong>Sesame aroma (芝麻香, GB/T 20824)</strong> — Shandong's Jingzhi. Despite the name there is no sesame in it; the toasted-nut character comes from high-temperature fermentation. The most savoury baijiu after sauce aroma.</li>
        <li><strong>Laobaigan aroma (老白干香, GB/T 20825)</strong> — Hebei's Hengshui Laobaigan. Extremely clean, high-proof, and dry, with a wheat-based fermentation that reads almost austere.</li>
        <li><strong>Chi aroma (豉香, GB/T 16289)</strong> — Guangdong's Yubingshao. Fermented with cooked pork fat in the jar, giving a fermented black-bean character. Genuinely strange to most palates and beloved in the Pearl River Delta.</li>
      </ul>

