      <section class="faq" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently asked</h2>
        <h3>Which aroma type is best for beginners?</h3>
        <p>Light aroma or a soft strong aroma. Both are legible to a palate trained on Western spirits, and both cost little enough that a disappointing bottle is not expensive. Sauce aroma is the worst starting point despite being the most famous.</p>
        <h3>Is Maotai a type of baijiu?</h3>
        <p>No — Maotai is a brand, and its products are sauce aroma. The confusion is understandable because Maotai dominates the category's reputation, but "sauce aroma" is the style and "Maotai" is one producer within it.</p>
        <h3>Does a higher price mean a better aroma type?</h3>
        <p>No. Aroma type predicts style, not quality. There are $20 strong-aroma bottles that outperform $100 ones, and there are mediocre sauce-aroma bottles at every price. Price in baijiu reflects scarcity, gifting demand, and brand power at least as much as liquid quality.</p>
        <h3>Can I tell the aroma type from the bottle?</h3>
        <p>Yes — the GB/T standard code on the back label identifies it. 10781.1 is strong, 10781.2 is light, 10781.3 is rice, 26760 is sauce. This is the most reliable information on the packaging and it is legally required.</p>
        <h3>What does "aroma type" translate to on a Chinese label?</h3>
        <p>香型 (xiāngxíng). You will see it written as 浓香型, 酱香型, 清香型 and so on. The character 型 means "type" and always follows the aroma name.</p>
      </section>

